CONVOLVE
++++++++

SUMMARY
-------
Compute the discrete convolution of a master signal with itself and with
all time series in memory.

SYNTAX
------
::

  CONVOLVE  {MASTER name|n},   {NUMBER n},{LENGTH ON|OFF|v},
      {TYPE  RECTANGLE|HAMMING|HANNING|COSINE|TRIANGLE}

INPUT
-----
   :MASTER name|n:  Select master file in data file list by name or
       number.  All files will be cornvolved with the MASTER.

   :AMPLITUDE OFF: No normalization.  Default

   :AMPLITUDE ON: Preserve amplitudes by multiplyig by DELTA

   :NUMBER n:  Set number of correlation windows to be used.

   :LENGTH ON:  Turn fixed window length option on.

   :LENGTH OFF:  Turn fixed window length option off.  Default

   :LENGTH v:  Turn fixed window length option on and change window
       length in seconds to v.

   :TYPE RECTANGLE:  Apply a rectangle function to each window.  This is
       equivalent to applying no function to each window.

   :TYPE HAMMING:  Apply a hamming function to each window.

   :TYPE HANNING:  Apply a hanning function to each window.

   :TYPE COSINE:  Apply a cosine function to each window.

   :TYPE TRIANGLE:  Apply a triangle function to each window.

DEFAULT VALUES
--------------
::

  CONVOLVE MASTER 1 NUMBER 1 LENGTH OFF TYPE RECTANGLE AMPLITUDE OFF

DESCRIPTION
-----------
  If there are N SAC files in memory, there are N output files that are the
  results of the convolution with MASTER (including the convolution of
  MASTER with itself).  All signals in memory must have the same DELTA. The
  output time series preserves amplitudes if one chooses AMPLITUDE ON.

  For a discrete convolution, only points are considered so the MASTER and
  all other time series in memory are effectively asssumed to start at the
  same time.  Hence, if one wants to convolve a waveform time series with a
  time-symmetric pulse that has a maximum at zero time (perhaps as a method
  of non-causal,low-pass filtering to smooth out Gibbs phenomenon that often
  accompanies arrival spikes in a synthetic waveform), the output time series
  will be time-shifted forward by half the width of a time-symmetric pulse.
  See the `Time-Series Convolution Primer` section in
  `Using the SAC Libraries`_ for further discussion of this point.

HEADER CHANGES
--------------
  DEPMIN, DEPMAX, DEPMEN


